WB.L("blood-types-explained", "body", "beginner", "Blood types: why your blood has a label", "For centuries, giving someone blood was a gamble that often killed them. Then a Viennese doctor mixed a few test tubes and discovered why.",
  [
    ["A deadly gamble", "Early transfusions were dangerous because nobody knew that blood comes in incompatible types.",
      "In 1667 the French physician Jean-Baptiste Denys transfused lamb's blood into human patients. A few seemed to survive, but after one man died the practice was banned in France, and transfusion fell out of favour for well over a century. From 1818 the English obstetrician James Blundell tried again, this time using human blood, notably for women bleeding heavily after childbirth. Some patients recovered remarkably. Others suffered fever, back pain, dark urine and death, and no one could predict which would happen. Doctors of the 1800s were left with a frustrating puzzle. The same procedure that rescued one dying patient killed the next, and the reason lay hidden in the blood itself, invisible to the microscopes of the day."],
    ["Landsteiner's test tubes", "In 1901 Karl Landsteiner showed that mixing some people's blood makes it clump, and identified the first blood groups.",
      "In 1901 Karl Landsteiner, working in Vienna, took blood from himself and his colleagues and separated it into red cells and serum, the liquid part. Then he mixed each person's serum with every other person's red cells. Sometimes nothing happened. Sometimes the red cells clumped together into sticky clusters, a reaction called agglutination. From the pattern he sorted people into groups, which became known as A, B and O. A year later two of his colleagues found the rarer fourth group, AB. The clumping explained the old disasters: incompatible donor cells were being attacked and destroyed inside the patient. Landsteiner's work made safe transfusion possible, and in 1930 it earned him the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."],
    ["Labels on your red cells", "Your blood type depends on which sugar markers, called antigens, coat your red blood cells.",
      "Every red blood cell is studded with molecules on its surface. In the ABO system, the important ones are chains of sugars. Type A cells carry the A antigen, type B cells carry B, type AB cells carry both, and type O cells carry neither. The twist is that your plasma usually contains antibodies against whichever antigens you lack. Someone with type A blood has anti B antibodies, and someone with type O has both anti A and anti B. These antibodies appear in early childhood, probably because gut bacteria carry similar sugar molecules that train the immune system. If incompatible blood is transfused, those antibodies latch onto the foreign cells and can trigger a severe, sometimes fatal, reaction."],
    ["The positive and negative part", "The Rh factor is a second major blood group system, and it matters greatly in pregnancy.",
      "The plus or minus after your blood type refers to another system, discovered in 1940 by Landsteiner and Alexander Wiener. They named it after the rhesus monkeys used in their experiments, and the name stuck even though the human antigen later proved to be different. People whose cells carry the main Rh antigen, called D, are Rh positive; those without it are Rh negative. This became vital in pregnancy. If an Rh negative mother carries an Rh positive baby, she can make antibodies that attack the red cells of that baby or a later one, a condition called haemolytic disease of the newborn. Since the late 1960s, injections of anti D antibodies have prevented most of these cases."],
    ["Universal donors", "O negative red cells can be given to almost anyone, which makes them precious in emergencies.",
      "Because type O negative red cells carry no A, B or D antigens, the recipient's antibodies have nothing to attack. That is why O negative is called the universal donor for red cells, and why ambulances and emergency departments rely on it when there is no time to test a patient's blood. At the other end, people with AB positive blood can receive red cells of any ABO and Rh type, because they make no anti A or anti B antibodies. For plasma the rules flip: AB plasma contains no ABO antibodies, so it can be given to anyone. Even with these shortcuts, hospitals still crossmatch blood whenever they can, testing donor and patient samples together before a planned transfusion."],
    ["More than four types", "Scientists now recognise more than 40 blood group systems, and blood type does not shape your personality.",
      "ABO and Rh are the systems that matter most for everyday transfusion, but they are far from the whole story. Researchers have identified more than 40 blood group systems and hundreds of individual antigens, which is why people with rare combinations sometimes need specially matched donors. In Australia, roughly four in ten people are O positive, making it the most common type, while O negative is carried by fewer than one in ten. AB negative is the rarest, at around one per cent. One popular idea deserves retiring. In Japan and some other countries, blood type is often linked to personality, much like star signs. Careful studies involving large numbers of people have found no meaningful connection."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "What did Karl Landsteiner observe when he mixed serum from one person with red cells from another?", ["The cells sometimes clumped together", "The mixture always turned clear", "The cells divided rapidly", "The serum turned solid"], 0, "Landsteiner saw agglutination, or clumping, in some combinations. The pattern revealed the A, B and O groups."],
    [2, "Why does a person with type A blood react badly to type B red cells?", ["Type B cells are larger", "Their plasma contains anti B antibodies", "Type A blood lacks platelets", "Type B cells carry no antigens"], 1, "People usually carry antibodies against the ABO antigens they lack, so type A plasma contains anti B antibodies that attack type B cells."],
    [3, "What problem did anti D injections largely prevent?", ["Blood clots after surgery", "Iron deficiency in pregnancy", "Haemolytic disease of the newborn", "Allergic reactions to plasma"], 2, "An Rh negative mother can make antibodies against an Rh positive baby's red cells. Anti D injections stop her immune system from becoming sensitised."],
    [4, "Why is O negative blood used in emergencies when there is no time to test?", ["It clots faster than other types", "It carries extra oxygen", "It is the most common type in Australia", "Its red cells lack A, B and D antigens"], 3, "With no A, B or D antigens on its red cells, O negative blood gives the recipient's antibodies nothing to target."],
    [5, "What have large studies found about blood type and personality?", ["Type O people are more confident", "There is no meaningful connection", "Type AB people are more creative", "The link only applies in Japan"], 1, "Despite the popular belief, careful research has not found any meaningful link between blood type and personality."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("how-wounds-heal", "body", "beginner", "How a cut heals", "Slice your finger and your body launches a carefully timed rescue operation, from emergency plugging to months of quiet rebuilding.",
  [
    ["Stopping the leak", "Within seconds, blood vessels narrow and platelets begin plugging the hole.",
      "The moment skin is cut, the damaged blood vessels tighten, reducing the flow of blood to the area. Tiny cell fragments called platelets, which drift through the blood in huge numbers, touch the exposed tissue and become sticky. They clump together into a soft plug and release chemicals that call in more platelets. At the same time a chain of clotting proteins in the plasma switches on, step by step, until a protein called fibrinogen is converted into long strands of fibrin. These strands weave through the platelet plug like a net, trapping red cells and turning liquid blood into a solid clot. As the surface of that clot dries out, it becomes the familiar crusty scab."],
    ["The four ancient signs", "Redness, heat, swelling and pain are signs that the clean-up crew has arrived.",
      "Around two thousand years ago the Roman writer Celsus described the four classic signs of inflammation: redness, heat, swelling and pain. They are not signs that something is going wrong. Damaged cells release chemical alarms that widen nearby blood vessels, bringing extra blood that makes the area red and warm. The vessel walls become leakier, so fluid and immune cells seep into the tissue, causing swelling. Within hours, white blood cells called neutrophils arrive to kill bacteria. They are followed by macrophages, large cells that swallow debris, dead cells and germs. Macrophages also release signals that tell the next wave of repair cells to get to work, which makes them the managers of healing."],
    ["Rebuilding the tissue", "Over the following days, new blood vessels, collagen and skin cells fill and close the wound.",
      "After a few days, the wound enters its building phase. Cells called fibroblasts move in and start producing collagen, the tough protein that gives skin its strength. New capillaries sprout into the area to supply oxygen and nutrients. Together they form granulation tissue, the pink, slightly bumpy surface you may see in a healing graze. Meanwhile, skin cells at the edges of the wound multiply and crawl across the gap, sliding underneath the scab until they meet in the middle. Some fibroblasts transform into specialised cells that can contract, gently pulling the wound edges closer together. When the new skin is complete, the scab is no longer needed and eventually falls away."],
    ["The long remodel", "Scar tissue keeps changing for a year or more, but it never fully matches the original skin.",
      "The final phase is the slowest. The first collagen laid down is hastily arranged, and over the following months the body breaks it down and replaces it with stronger, better organised fibres. A scar that looked red and raised often fades and flattens over a year or more. Even so, scar tissue never becomes perfect skin. It typically regains at most around 80 per cent of the original strength, and it usually lacks hair follicles and sweat glands. Curiously, researchers have found that wounds in early foetuses can heal without any scar at all. Understanding exactly how they do it is one of the long-standing goals of wound research."],
    ["Let it breathe?", "Keeping a wound clean and moist usually helps it heal faster than leaving it to dry in the air.",
      "Many of us grew up hearing that cuts should be left uncovered so they can breathe. In 1962 a British researcher named George Winter tested this on pigs, whose skin is similar to ours. Shallow wounds covered with a plastic film, which kept them moist, healed considerably faster than wounds left exposed to the air. A dry scab, it turns out, is a barrier that new skin cells must tunnel underneath. A moist surface lets them glide across more easily. Winter's work helped launch modern wound dressings, from simple hydrocolloid patches to specialised hospital products. The practical lesson for small cuts is to clean them gently and keep them covered."],
    ["What slows healing", "Smoking, diabetes, poor nutrition and infection can all delay repair.",
      "Healing needs a good blood supply, oxygen and raw materials, so anything that interferes with those slows the process. Smoking narrows blood vessels and reduces oxygen delivery. Diabetes can damage circulation and nerves, which is why people with diabetes are advised to check their feet carefully. Protein and vitamin C are essential for making collagen. Sailors with scurvy, caused by a lack of vitamin C, were recorded as having old, long healed wounds break open again. Healing also slows with age. Get medical help for wounds that are deep, gaping, very dirty or caused by animal bites, or that show signs of spreading infection. For severe bleeding, apply firm pressure and call 000."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What protein forms the net of strands that turns liquid blood into a clot?", ["Collagen", "Keratin", "Fibrin", "Haemoglobin"], 2, "Clotting proteins convert fibrinogen into fibrin, whose strands weave through the platelet plug and trap blood cells."],
    [1, "Why does an injured area become red and warm?", ["Nearby blood vessels widen and bring in more blood", "Bacteria produce heat as they grow", "Nerves overheat from pain signals", "The scab traps body heat"], 0, "Damaged cells release chemicals that widen vessels. Extra blood flow produces the redness and warmth of inflammation."],
    [2, "Which cells produce the collagen that rebuilds the wound?", ["Neutrophils", "Fibroblasts", "Platelets", "Red blood cells"], 1, "Fibroblasts move into the wound and lay down collagen, forming granulation tissue together with new capillaries."],
    [3, "How does a mature scar compare with the original skin?", ["It is stronger than the original", "It is identical once fully healed", "It contains extra sweat glands", "It is weaker and usually lacks hair follicles"], 3, "Scar tissue usually regains at most about 80 per cent of the original strength and lacks hair follicles and sweat glands."],
    [4, "What did George Winter's 1962 experiment show?", ["Moist, covered wounds healed faster than exposed ones", "Wounds heal fastest in direct sunlight", "Scabs speed up healing", "Covering wounds causes infection"], 0, "Winter found that wounds kept moist under film healed faster, because new skin cells can move more easily across a moist surface."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("teeth-enamel-decay", "body", "beginner", "Your teeth: the hardest part of you", "Your teeth are coated in the toughest material your body makes, yet a bag of lollies can slowly dissolve it. Here is how they work and how to keep them.",
  [
    ["Built in layers", "Teeth have a hard outer shell of enamel, a living layer of dentine and a soft pulp inside.",
      "A tooth is built like a fortress. The outer layer of the crown is enamel, the hardest substance in the human body. It is about 96 per cent mineral, mostly crystals of a calcium phosphate called hydroxyapatite. Beneath it lies dentine, which is softer and full of microscopic tubes. That is why a worn or cracked tooth can feel sensitive to cold. At the centre is the pulp, a soft core of nerves and blood vessels that keeps the tooth alive. The root is anchored into the jawbone by tiny fibres called the periodontal ligament. Enamel has one great weakness. It contains no living cells, so once it is lost, the body cannot grow it back."],
    ["Two sets for a lifetime", "Humans grow 20 baby teeth, then 32 adult teeth that must last the rest of their lives.",
      "Most mammals, including us, get two sets of teeth. Children have 20 baby teeth, which usually start appearing at around six months. Even then, the adult teeth are already forming deep in the jaw. From about the age of six, the baby teeth loosen and fall out as the permanent teeth push up from below. A full adult set has 32 teeth: incisors for cutting, canines for tearing, premolars and molars for grinding. The last four molars, known as wisdom teeth, usually arrive between the late teens and mid twenties, if they arrive at all. Unlike sharks, which replace teeth throughout their lives, we get no third set."],
    ["How decay happens", "Mouth bacteria turn sugar into acid, and that acid dissolves enamel.",
      "Your mouth is home to hundreds of species of bacteria, which live in a sticky film called plaque. Some of them, including a species called Streptococcus mutans, feed on sugars and release acid as waste. When the surface of a tooth becomes acidic enough, roughly below a pH of 5.5, minerals start to dissolve out of the enamel. Saliva fights back. It neutralises acid and supplies calcium and phosphate that can rebuild early damage. Trouble starts when the attacks come too often for saliva to keep up. That is why how often you eat sugar matters more than the total amount. Sipping soft drink all afternoon is harder on teeth than having one with lunch."],
    ["The mystery of the brown stain", "Fluoride was discovered when a dentist investigated mottled teeth that rarely decayed.",
      "In the early 1900s a young dentist named Frederick McKay moved to Colorado Springs and noticed that many locals had brown stains on their teeth. Over years of investigation he found the stained teeth were strangely resistant to decay, and the cause was eventually traced to naturally high levels of fluoride in the water. Researchers then worked out that a much lower level could reduce decay without staining. In 1945 Grand Rapids, Michigan, became the first city to add fluoride to its water supply deliberately. In Australia, the Tasmanian town of Beaconsfield led the way in 1953. Fluoride helps enamel rebuild into a form called fluorapatite, which resists acid better than the original."],
    ["Gums hold it all together", "Gum disease starts with plaque and, left untreated, can destroy the bone that holds teeth in place.",
      "Healthy teeth can still be lost if the gums and bone around them fail. Plaque that is not removed along the gumline irritates the gums, causing gingivitis, with redness, swelling and bleeding when you brush. At this stage the damage is reversible with good cleaning. Plaque left in place hardens into tartar, which a toothbrush cannot remove. Over time, ongoing inflammation can progress to periodontitis, in which pockets form between gums and teeth and the supporting bone is gradually destroyed. Teeth can loosen and eventually fall out. Researchers have found links between gum disease and conditions such as diabetes and heart disease, though how much one directly causes the other is still being studied."],
    ["From chew sticks to nylon", "People have cleaned their teeth for thousands of years, but the modern toothbrush is surprisingly recent.",
      "Ancient peoples cleaned their teeth with chew sticks, twigs frayed at one end, and the miswak stick is still widely used in parts of the Middle East, Africa and Asia. Bristle brushes made with hog hair appeared in China centuries ago. In England, a man named William Addis began mass producing toothbrushes in the late 1700s; popular accounts say he came up with the idea while in prison. The first nylon bristle brushes went on sale in 1938, replacing animal hair. Today, dental authorities, including in Australia, recommend brushing twice a day with fluoride toothpaste, cleaning between the teeth, and spitting out rather than rinsing, so some fluoride stays on the enamel."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why can't the body repair enamel that has been lost?", ["Enamel is made of protein", "Enamel contains no living cells", "Enamel has no minerals", "Enamel is replaced every year"], 1, "Enamel is almost entirely mineral and has no living cells, so the body cannot regrow it once it is gone."],
    [1, "How many teeth are in a full adult set?", ["20", "28", "36", "32"], 3, "Adults have 32 teeth, including four wisdom teeth. Children have 20 baby teeth."],
    [2, "Why is sipping a sugary drink all afternoon worse than drinking it with a meal?", ["Repeated acid attacks give saliva no time to repair enamel", "Sugar becomes more acidic over time", "Saliva stops flowing after meals", "It stains the teeth brown"], 0, "Frequent sugar keeps the mouth acidic. Saliva needs time between attacks to neutralise acid and rebuild minerals."],
    [3, "What clue led to the discovery of fluoride's benefits?", ["Teeth that glowed under ultraviolet light", "Children who never lost baby teeth", "Brown stained teeth that rarely decayed", "Sailors whose teeth fell out"], 2, "Frederick McKay noticed that mottled, stained teeth in Colorado Springs resisted decay, which was traced to fluoride in the water."],
    [4, "What is true of gingivitis?", ["It always leads to tooth loss", "It is reversible with good cleaning", "It is caused by fluoride", "It only affects baby teeth"], 1, "Gingivitis, the early stage of gum disease, can be reversed by removing plaque. Untreated, it can progress to periodontitis."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("how-your-voice-works", "body", "beginner", "How your voice works", "Every word you speak begins as a buzz from two tiny folds of tissue in your throat. The rest is shaped by your body like a musical instrument.",
  [
    ["The voice box", "Your voice is made in the larynx, where two vocal folds sit across the airway.",
      "Sitting at the top of your windpipe is the larynx, or voice box, a structure of cartilage, muscle and ligament. The bump at the front of the neck, often called the Adam's apple, is part of its largest cartilage, and it tends to stick out more in men. Inside, stretched across the airway, are two vocal folds. They are often called vocal cords, but they are not strings. Each is a layered band of muscle and ligament covered in soft, moist membrane. When you breathe, the folds swing apart to let air through. When you swallow, they snap shut, helping to stop food and drink going down the wrong way. When you speak, they come together and vibrate."],
    ["Making the buzz", "Air from your lungs forces the vocal folds open and shut hundreds of times every second.",
      "To make a sound, muscles bring the vocal folds together. Air pushed up from the lungs builds pressure beneath them until it forces them apart and a puff escapes. The folds then spring back together, helped by their own elasticity and the drop in pressure as air rushes between them, and the cycle repeats. This happens remarkably fast. A typical adult man's speaking voice vibrates at around 100 to 120 cycles per second, and a typical woman's at around 200. Tighter, thinner folds vibrate faster and produce higher notes, which is how you change pitch. Push more air through and the sound gets louder. On its own, though, this buzz sounds nothing like speech."],
    ["Shaping the sound", "Your throat, mouth and nose act as a resonating chamber that turns a buzz into words.",
      "The raw buzz from the larynx travels up through the throat, mouth and nose, which together form the vocal tract. Like the body of a guitar, this space strengthens some frequencies and weakens others. By moving your tongue, lips, jaw and soft palate you change its shape, and with it the sound. Vowels depend mainly on tongue and lip position; say ‘ee’ then ‘oo’ and feel the difference. Consonants come from briefly blocking or narrowing the airflow with the lips, teeth or tongue. Whispering shows the two systems working separately. When you whisper, your vocal folds do not vibrate at all, yet you can still form clear words using the air rushing through your mouth."],
    ["Why voices change", "Puberty, hormones and age all reshape the voice box and the sounds it makes.",
      "Children of both sexes have similar voices because their larynxes are similar in size. At puberty, testosterone makes the larynx grow and the vocal folds lengthen and thicken, especially in boys. Longer, heavier folds vibrate more slowly, and many male voices drop by around an octave. During the change, boys may find their voice cracks unpredictably as the muscles adjust to a rapidly changing instrument. Girls' voices deepen too, but only slightly. Voices keep changing throughout life. In older age, vocal folds can thin and lose some muscle, which can make a voice sound weaker or breathier. Hormonal changes around menopause can also lower a woman's voice a little."],
    ["Hearing yourself", "Your recorded voice sounds strange because you normally hear yourself partly through your bones.",
      "Most people wince when they hear a recording of their own voice. There is a physical reason. When others hear you, the sound reaches them only through the air. When you hear yourself speak, sound also travels to your inner ears through the bones of your skull. Bone conduction carries lower frequencies well, so the voice in your head sounds deeper and fuller than the one everyone else hears. A recording captures only the air carried version, which can sound thin and unfamiliar. Psychology may add to the discomfort, because the voice is so tied to our sense of self. The voice on the recording, though, is much closer to what other people actually hear."],
    ["Looking after your voice", "Vocal folds can be strained by overuse, and lasting hoarseness should be checked by a doctor.",
      "Because the vocal folds collide many times every second, heavy use takes a toll. Teachers, singers, coaches and call centre workers are among those most likely to develop voice problems. Repeated strain can cause swelling or small, callus-like growths called vocal nodules, which make the voice rough or tired. Common advice includes resting your voice when it is sore, avoiding habitual shouting or straining to be heard over noise, staying well hydrated and not smoking. Whispering is not necessarily restful, as many people strain while doing it. Hoarseness from a cold usually settles within a week or two, but hoarseness lasting more than about three weeks should be checked by a doctor."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What happens to the vocal folds when you swallow?", ["They vibrate rapidly", "They close to help protect the airway", "They stretch to their longest", "They swing wide open"], 1, "The folds snap shut during swallowing, which helps stop food and drink from entering the airway."],
    [1, "How do you raise the pitch of your voice?", ["By pushing more air through", "By opening your mouth wider", "By tightening and thinning the vocal folds", "By breathing through your nose"], 2, "Tighter, thinner folds vibrate faster, producing higher notes. More air mainly makes the sound louder."],
    [2, "What is unusual about whispering?", ["The vocal folds do not vibrate", "It uses only the nose", "The vocal folds vibrate fastest", "It needs no airflow"], 0, "In a whisper the folds stay still. Words are formed by air rushing through the shaped mouth and throat."],
    [3, "Why do many male voices drop at puberty?", ["The lungs grow larger", "The tongue becomes heavier", "The nose lengthens", "Testosterone makes the vocal folds longer and thicker"], 3, "Testosterone causes the larynx to grow and the folds to lengthen and thicken, so they vibrate more slowly."],
    [4, "Why does your voice sound deeper to you than on a recording?", ["Microphones remove low notes", "You also hear it through the bones of your skull", "Your ears are tuned to high sounds", "Recordings speed up speech"], 1, "Bone conduction carries lower frequencies to your inner ear, so your own voice sounds fuller in your head than in the air."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("water-and-thirst", "body", "beginner", "Water and thirst: how much do you really need?", "Eight glasses a day sounds like ancient wisdom, but its origins are murkier than you might think. Your body has a far better guide built in.",
  [
    ["A body made of water", "Water makes up roughly half to 60 per cent of an adult's body weight.",
      "Water is the most abundant substance in your body. In a typical adult it accounts for roughly 50 to 60 per cent of body weight, and in newborn babies the share is higher still, at around 75 per cent. People with more muscle tend to carry more water, because muscle holds more water than fat does. About two thirds of it sits inside your cells, and the rest bathes the spaces between cells or flows as blood plasma. Water dissolves nutrients, carries waste to the kidneys, cushions joints and the brain, and spreads heat around the body. Almost every chemical reaction that keeps you alive takes place in it, which is why your body guards its water levels so carefully."],
    ["Coming and going", "You lose water constantly through urine, sweat and breath, and food supplies a good share of what you take in.",
      "Even on a quiet day you are always losing water. Most leaves as urine, but a surprising amount escapes unnoticed as sweat evaporating from your skin and as water vapour in every breath you exhale. More is lost in faeces. A sedentary adult in a mild climate typically loses around two and a half litres a day, and far more during hard exercise or hot weather. To balance this, water comes in from drinks, from food and in a small amount from the chemical reactions that burn fuel inside your cells. Food matters more than many people realise. Fruit, vegetables, soups, rice and pasta are full of water, and food can supply roughly a fifth of a typical intake."],
    ["The eight glasses myth", "There is no good evidence that everyone needs eight glasses of plain water a day.",
      "The rule of eight glasses a day is repeated everywhere, but nobody has found solid scientific evidence behind it. One often-cited origin is a 1945 recommendation from a United States nutrition board, which suggested about two and a half litres of water a day for adults. The same advice added that most of this is already contained in prepared foods, a detail that was quietly forgotten. Australian guidelines suggest typical fluid intakes from drinks of around 2.6 litres for men and 2.1 litres for women, but these are averages for healthy people, not targets everyone must hit. Tea, coffee, milk and other drinks all count. The mild diuretic effect of caffeine is small in regular drinkers."],
    ["Your built-in sensor", "The brain monitors how concentrated your blood is and adjusts thirst and urine to match.",
      "Deep in the brain, the hypothalamus contains sensors that track how concentrated your blood is. When you lose water, the blood becomes slightly saltier, and these sensors respond in two ways. They make you feel thirsty, and they prompt the pituitary gland to release antidiuretic hormone, also called vasopressin. This hormone tells the kidneys to reabsorb more water, so your urine becomes smaller in volume and darker in colour. When you drink plenty, the hormone falls and the kidneys release more dilute urine. The system is sensitive, responding to changes of only a few per cent. For most healthy people, drinking when thirsty and noticing pale straw-coloured urine is a reliable guide."],
    ["When thirst is not enough", "Older adults, babies and people working in heat are more vulnerable to dehydration.",
      "Thirst works well for most healthy adults, but not for everyone. The thirst response tends to weaken with age, so older people can become dehydrated without feeling particularly thirsty, especially when they are unwell. Babies and young children lose fluid quickly through fever, vomiting or diarrhoea and cannot fetch a drink for themselves. People doing hard physical work or exercise in Australian summer heat can sweat out more than a litre an hour. Early signs of dehydration include thirst, dark urine, headache, tiredness and dizziness. Heat stroke is a medical emergency. If someone in the heat becomes confused, collapses or stops making sense, call 000 and start cooling them while you wait."],
    ["Too much of a good thing", "Drinking far more than you need can dangerously dilute the sodium in your blood.",
      "It is possible to drink too much water. If you take in fluid much faster than your kidneys can remove it, the sodium in your blood becomes diluted, a condition called hyponatraemia. Water moves into cells, including brain cells, which can swell. Symptoms range from nausea and headache to confusion, seizures and, rarely, death. Endurance athletes are among those at risk, particularly slower runners who drink at every station for hours. A study of Boston Marathon runners, published in 2005, found that around one in eight had low blood sodium at the finish, and weight gain from drinking too much was the strongest predictor. Many sports doctors now advise athletes to drink to thirst rather than forcing fluids."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Roughly what share of an adult's body weight is water?", ["10 to 20 per cent", "50 to 60 per cent", "80 to 90 per cent", "About 30 per cent"], 1, "Water makes up roughly 50 to 60 per cent of an adult's body weight, and an even higher share in newborn babies."],
    [1, "Which of these is a source of water that people often overlook?", ["Food such as fruit, vegetables and soups", "Air absorbed through the skin", "Breathing in through the nose", "Stored water in the bones"], 0, "Food can provide roughly a fifth of daily water intake, and the body also makes a little water when burning fuel."],
    [2, "What detail of the 1945 recommendation was later forgotten?", ["It applied only to children", "It was meant for soldiers only", "Most of the water is contained in prepared foods", "It recommended 20 glasses a day"], 2, "The advice said most of the suggested amount is already in food, a caveat that disappeared as the ‘eight glasses’ idea spread."],
    [3, "What does antidiuretic hormone do when you are low on water?", ["It makes you sweat more", "It speeds up breathing", "It raises blood sugar", "It tells the kidneys to keep more water"], 3, "Antidiuretic hormone, or vasopressin, makes the kidneys reabsorb water, producing smaller amounts of darker urine."],
    [5, "What is hyponatraemia?", ["Dangerously diluted sodium in the blood", "A shortage of red blood cells", "A kidney infection caused by dehydration", "Very high blood sugar"], 0, "Drinking far more water than the kidneys can clear dilutes blood sodium, which can make cells, including brain cells, swell."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("fever-explained", "body", "intermediate", "Fever: why your body turns up the heat", "A fever is not your body losing control of its temperature. It is a deliberate decision, and one that animals have been making for hundreds of millions of years.",
  [
    ["Resetting the thermostat", "In a fever, the brain deliberately raises the temperature it aims to maintain.",
      "Your body temperature is controlled by the hypothalamus, a small region at the base of the brain that works much like a thermostat. During an infection, immune cells release signalling molecules, including cytokines such as interleukin 1 and interleukin 6. These trigger the production of a substance called prostaglandin E2, which acts on the hypothalamus and raises its set point. Suddenly your normal temperature feels too cold. You shiver, your skin turns pale as blood vessels narrow to save heat, and you reach for a blanket, all to warm yourself up to the new target. When the fever breaks and the set point drops again, the opposite happens. You feel hot, flush and sweat heavily."],
    ["The myth of 37 degrees", "Normal body temperature is a range, not a single number, and it changes throughout the day.",
      "The figure of 37 degrees Celsius traces back largely to the German physician Carl Wunderlich, who in 1868 published an analysis of an enormous number of armpit temperature readings. His work helped make the thermometer a standard medical tool. Modern measurements suggest the average is often a little lower, commonly somewhere around 36.5 to 36.8 degrees, though it depends on the person and where the temperature is measured. Your temperature also rises and falls with your body clock, usually lowest in the early morning and highest in the late afternoon or evening. Age, activity and, for women, the menstrual cycle shift it too. Some researchers have even found evidence that average temperatures have drifted down since Wunderlich's day."],
    ["Lizards with a fever", "Cold-blooded animals give themselves a fever by seeking warmth, which suggests fever is ancient and useful.",
      "In the 1970s the physiologist Matthew Kluger ran experiments with desert iguanas, which cannot make their own body heat and instead control their temperature by moving between warm and cool spots. When infected with bacteria, the lizards chose warmer places, raising their temperature by around two degrees, a so-called behavioural fever. Lizards kept at cooler temperatures were much more likely to die. Similar behaviour has since been seen in fish, insects and other animals. Because fever appears across so many branches of the animal kingdom, and producing it costs a lot of energy, most scientists conclude it evolved because it helps fight infection. A costly trait that useless would likely have been lost long ago."],
    ["How heat helps", "Higher temperatures boost parts of the immune system and make life harder for some germs.",
      "Fever seems to help in several ways. Many immune processes run faster at higher temperatures, including the movement of white blood cells and the production of some defensive molecules. Some bacteria and viruses multiply less efficiently when their surroundings are warmer, and the body also restricts the iron that many bacteria need to grow. History offers a grim illustration of heat's power. In 1917 the Austrian psychiatrist Julius Wagner-Jauregg deliberately infected patients with neurosyphilis, a then incurable brain infection, with malaria to induce high fevers. Some patients improved, and he received the Nobel Prize in 1927. The treatment was dangerous and was abandoned once penicillin arrived, but it showed fever could attack a disease."],
    ["Fever is not heat stroke", "A fever is controlled by the brain, whereas heat stroke is the body failing to shed heat.",
      "A fever and heat stroke can both produce a high temperature, but they are very different. In a fever, the brain has raised its set point and the body is still regulating carefully around it. Fevers from infection rarely climb much above about 41 degrees, because the system keeps working. In heat stroke, the set point has not changed. Instead, the body is absorbing or producing heat faster than it can get rid of it, perhaps during hard work in a heatwave, and its temperature keeps rising out of control. Medicines that lower fever work on the set point, so they do not treat heat stroke. Heat stroke is an emergency: call 000 and begin cooling the person immediately."],
    ["When to get help", "Most fevers are harmless, but some warning signs need urgent medical attention.",
      "Most fevers are the body doing its job and pass within a few days. Medicines that lower temperature mainly make people feel more comfortable and do not cure the underlying illness, so follow the label or ask a pharmacist. Some situations, however, call for prompt medical help. Babies under three months old with a fever should be seen by a doctor urgently. Seek help quickly for anyone with a fever who also has a stiff neck, a rash that does not fade when pressed, difficulty breathing, severe drowsiness or confusion. Young children sometimes have a seizure with a fever, called a febrile convulsion. These are frightening but usually harmless. Call 000 if a seizure lasts more than five minutes."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does a person with a rising fever shiver and feel cold?", ["Their blood has lost heat to the infection", "The brain has raised its target temperature", "The infection has damaged their nerves", "Their muscles are too weak to stay warm"], 1, "The hypothalamus raises its set point, so normal body temperature suddenly feels too cold and the body works to warm up."],
    [1, "What do modern measurements say about normal body temperature?", ["It is exactly 37 degrees for everyone", "It never changes during the day", "It is a range, often a little below 37 degrees", "It is always higher in the morning"], 2, "Normal temperature varies by person, time of day and measurement site, and the average is often a little below 37 degrees."],
    [2, "What did Matthew Kluger's experiments with desert iguanas show?", ["Infected lizards sought warmth, and cooler lizards died more often", "Lizards cannot get infections", "Fever kills cold-blooded animals", "Lizards cool down when infected"], 0, "Infected iguanas raised their temperature by moving somewhere warmer, and those kept cool had higher death rates."],
    [3, "Why did Julius Wagner-Jauregg infect patients with malaria?", ["To test a new vaccine", "To cure malaria", "To study mosquitoes", "To induce fevers that attacked neurosyphilis"], 3, "He used malaria-induced fevers to treat neurosyphilis, an approach abandoned once penicillin became available."],
    [4, "Why don't fever-reducing medicines treat heat stroke?", ["They only work on children", "Heat stroke is not caused by a raised set point", "They make heat stroke worse", "Heat stroke involves a low temperature"], 1, "These medicines lower the brain's set point. In heat stroke the set point is unchanged; the body simply cannot shed heat."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("reflexes-spinal-cord", "body", "intermediate", "Reflexes: when your spine decides first", "Touch a hot pan and your hand is gone before you feel the pain. That split-second move is made without your brain's permission.",
  [
    ["Descartes and the fire", "The idea that the body can react automatically goes back at least to René Descartes.",
      "In his book Treatise of Man, published in 1664 after his death, the French philosopher René Descartes included a famous drawing of a boy with his foot near a fire. Descartes imagined that the heat tugged on tiny threads inside the nerves, opening valves in the brain that sent spirits back to the muscles to pull the foot away. The details were wrong, but the core idea was striking. Some actions might happen mechanically, like clockwork, without any conscious choice. Over the following centuries, scientists discovered that many of these automatic responses do not even need the brain. They are handled lower down, in the spinal cord, by circuits we now call reflex arcs."],
    ["The knee jerk", "Tapping below the kneecap triggers one of the simplest circuits in the nervous system.",
      "When a doctor taps the tendon just below your kneecap, your lower leg kicks forward. In 1875 two German neurologists, Wilhelm Erb and Carl Westphal, independently described this reflex, and it has been tested ever since. The tap briefly stretches the large quadriceps muscle at the front of the thigh. Stretch sensors inside the muscle, called muscle spindles, fire signals along a sensory nerve into the spinal cord. There, the sensory neuron connects directly to a motor neuron, which sends a command straight back to the same muscle, telling it to contract. With only one connection in the chain, it is called a monosynaptic reflex. Its job in everyday life is to help keep you upright."],
    ["The anatomy of a reflex", "Every reflex arc has a sensor, an incoming nerve, a processing point, an outgoing nerve and a muscle.",
      "A reflex arc has five basic parts. A receptor detects a change, such as stretch, heat or pressure. A sensory neuron carries the signal to the spinal cord or brainstem. A processing step links that signal to an outgoing motor neuron, sometimes through extra nerve cells called interneurons. Finally a muscle or gland responds. The British physiologist Charles Sherrington spent decades mapping these circuits and coined the word synapse for the gap between nerve cells. He showed that reflexes coordinate muscles in pairs. When the quadriceps contracts during the knee jerk, signals simultaneously relax the hamstrings behind the thigh so they do not fight the movement. Sherrington shared the Nobel Prize in 1932."],
    ["Pulling back from danger", "The withdrawal reflex moves you away from harm and shifts your balance at the same time.",
      "Step on a sharp object and your foot lifts before you have time to think. This withdrawal reflex is more complex than the knee jerk, using several interneurons in the spinal cord to activate a whole group of muscles that pull the limb away. At the same moment, a linked circuit called the crossed extensor reflex stiffens your other leg so it can take your weight and you do not topple over. Signals also travel up to the brain, which is why you then feel the pain, look down and perhaps say something unprintable. Because the reflex circuit is short and the conscious experience of pain takes longer to form, the movement often comes first."],
    ["Reflexes in babies", "Newborns have special reflexes that fade as the brain matures, which makes them useful health checks.",
      "Babies are born with a set of reflexes that disappear within months. Stroke a newborn's cheek and they turn towards it, ready to feed, which is the rooting reflex. Put a finger in their palm and they grip it surprisingly hard. If they sense they are falling, they fling out their arms then bring them in, the Moro reflex. As the brain's higher regions develop, they take control and suppress these responses. Doctors use this to check development. In 1896 the French neurologist Joseph Babinski described how stroking the sole of the foot makes the big toe curl down in healthy adults. If it instead points upwards and the toes fan out, it can signal damage to nerve pathways from the brain."],
    ["Reflex versus reaction", "True reflexes take only tens of milliseconds, while conscious reactions take much longer.",
      "A simple spinal reflex like the knee jerk takes only a few tens of milliseconds from tap to twitch. A conscious reaction is much slower, because the signal must travel to the brain, be recognised and trigger a decision. Responding to something you see typically takes around a fifth to a quarter of a second. This difference shapes elite sport. In sprinting, a runner who reacts within a tenth of a second of the starting gun is treated as having false started, on the basis that a genuine response cannot reliably be that fast. Skills practised for years, such as catching a ball, can feel like reflexes, but they are learned, automatic movements rather than true reflex arcs."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "In the knee-jerk reflex, what does the sensory neuron connect to in the spinal cord?", ["Directly to a motor neuron", "A long chain of brain cells", "The heart's pacemaker", "The bones of the knee"], 0, "The knee jerk is monosynaptic: the sensory neuron connects straight to a motor neuron that makes the quadriceps contract."],
    [2, "What did Charles Sherrington show about muscles during the knee jerk?", ["Both thigh muscles contract together", "The hamstrings relax while the quadriceps contracts", "Only the brain can relax a muscle", "Muscles cannot work in pairs"], 1, "Sherrington showed reflexes coordinate opposing muscles, relaxing the hamstrings so they do not resist the movement."],
    [3, "What does the crossed extensor reflex do when you step on something sharp?", ["It makes you feel the pain sooner", "It lifts both feet at once", "It stiffens the other leg to support your weight", "It slows your heart rate"], 2, "While one leg withdraws, the crossed extensor reflex stiffens the opposite leg so you keep your balance."],
    [4, "In a healthy adult, what does the big toe normally do when the sole is stroked?", ["Points upward with toes fanning out", "Stays completely still", "Twitches sideways", "Curls downward"], 3, "In healthy adults the toe curls down. An upward toe with fanning toes, Babinski's sign, can signal damage to brain-to-spine pathways."],
    [5, "Why is a sprinter who reacts within a tenth of a second of the gun counted as false starting?", ["A genuine response cannot reliably be that fast", "Reflexes are banned in athletics", "The gun's sound takes longer to arrive", "It gives other runners a headache"], 0, "Conscious reactions take longer than a tenth of a second, so anything faster is treated as anticipation rather than a response."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("lymphatic-system", "body", "intermediate", "The lymphatic system: your hidden drainage network", "Alongside your blood vessels runs a second network that most people barely know exists. Without it, you would swell up like a balloon.",
  [
    ["The leak problem", "Fluid constantly leaks out of your blood vessels, and the lymphatic system returns it.",
      "Your smallest blood vessels, the capillaries, are deliberately leaky. As blood flows through them, pressure pushes plasma out into the tissues, carrying oxygen and nutrients to the cells. Most of this fluid seeps back into the capillaries, but not all of it. Each day several litres are left behind in the spaces between cells. If nothing collected it, your tissues would swell and your blood volume would steadily fall. That is the job of the lymphatic system, a network of thin-walled vessels that begin as tiny dead-end tubes woven among the tissues. They soak up the excess fluid, now called lymph, along with proteins, debris and sometimes germs, and carry it back towards the bloodstream."],
    ["Milky veins in a dog", "In 1622 an Italian anatomist spotted strange white vessels and opened up a new branch of anatomy.",
      "In 1622 the Italian anatomist Gaspare Aselli was dissecting a dog that had recently eaten when he noticed thin white threads running across its intestines. When he cut one, a milky fluid leaked out. He called them lacteals, from the Latin word for milk, and at first he thought they carried food to the liver. A few decades later the French anatomist Jean Pecquet traced the fluid to a large collecting channel in the chest, now called the thoracic duct. In the 1650s Olaus Rudbeck in Sweden and Thomas Bartholin in Denmark both described the wider lymphatic network and argued bitterly over who deserved credit. Together they revealed a whole circulatory system that had been hiding in plain sight."],
    ["A system with no pump", "Lymph is moved by muscle movement, breathing and one-way valves rather than by a heart.",
      "Blood has the heart to push it around, but lymph has no central pump. Instead, lymphatic vessels contain one-way valves, like those in veins, that stop fluid flowing backwards. The walls of larger lymph vessels can contract rhythmically, and every time nearby skeletal muscles squeeze, or your chest rises and falls as you breathe, lymph is nudged a little further along. Eventually most of it drains through the thoracic duct into large veins near the left collarbone, rejoining the blood. This helps explain why ankles often swell after hours of sitting still on a long flight. With the leg muscles inactive, the fluid is moved along more slowly, and gravity lets it pool in the feet."],
    ["Checkpoints for germs", "Hundreds of lymph nodes filter the fluid and act as meeting points for immune cells.",
      "Along the lymphatic vessels sit lymph nodes, small bean-shaped structures found in clusters in places such as the neck, armpits and groin. Estimates vary, but a typical adult has several hundred of them. As lymph passes through, the nodes filter out debris and microbes. They are also packed with lymphocytes, the immune cells that recognise specific invaders. Because fluid from every tissue flows through them, nodes act as checkpoints where immune cells encounter evidence of infection. When they detect a threat, the lymphocytes multiply rapidly, which is why the glands in your neck can become swollen and tender during a sore throat. The tonsils, the spleen and the thymus are also part of this wider lymphatic family."],
    ["A back door for fat", "Fat from your meals enters the body through the lymphatic system rather than directly into the blood.",
      "Aselli's milky vessels had a real purpose. Most nutrients absorbed from the small intestine travel straight into blood vessels that lead to the liver. Fats are different. After digestion, they are packaged inside intestinal cells into particles called chylomicrons, which are too large to pass easily into the blood capillaries. Instead they enter the lacteals, the lymphatic vessels inside the tiny finger-like projections lining the intestine. After a fatty meal, the lymph from the gut turns cloudy and white, just as Aselli saw. This fat-rich fluid travels up the thoracic duct and enters the bloodstream near the collarbone, reaching the rest of the body before it passes through the liver."],
    ["When drainage fails", "Damage to the lymphatic system can cause long-lasting swelling called lymphoedema.",
      "When lymphatic vessels are blocked or damaged, fluid builds up and causes lymphoedema, a persistent swelling, often in an arm or leg. In many countries, including Australia, a common cause is cancer treatment, because surgery or radiation may remove or damage lymph nodes. Cancers can spread through lymph vessels, so surgeons often check the first node a tumour drains into, called the sentinel node. In parts of the tropics, a major cause is lymphatic filariasis, in which parasitic worms spread by mosquitoes lodge in lymph vessels. In severe cases it causes enormous swelling known as elephantiasis. Mass treatment campaigns supported by the World Health Organization have eliminated it as a public health problem in a number of countries."],
    ["A surprise in the brain", "In 2015 researchers found lymphatic vessels in the coverings of the brain, overturning textbook teaching.",
      "For a long time, textbooks taught that the brain had no lymphatic vessels at all. Then, in 2015, two research teams, one in the United States and one in Finland, independently reported lymphatic vessels running through the meninges, the protective membranes that wrap the brain. The finding was startling because anatomists had studied the brain intensively for centuries without recognising them. These vessels appear to help drain fluid, waste and immune cells from around the brain into lymph nodes in the neck. Researchers are now investigating whether problems with this drainage play a role in conditions such as Alzheimer's disease, though much remains uncertain. It is a reminder that even well-mapped bodies still hold secrets."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does the body need a lymphatic system?", ["To pump blood when the heart rests", "To collect fluid that leaks out of capillaries and is not reabsorbed", "To produce red blood cells", "To carry oxygen to the brain"], 1, "Several litres of fluid are left in the tissues each day. Lymphatic vessels collect it and return it to the bloodstream."],
    [2, "What moves lymph through the body?", ["A small heart in the chest", "The kidneys", "Muscle movement, breathing and one-way valves", "Gravity alone"], 2, "With no central pump, lymph is pushed by surrounding muscles, breathing movements and vessel walls, while valves keep it moving one way."],
    [3, "Why can glands in your neck swell during a throat infection?", ["Immune cells in the lymph nodes multiply to fight it", "Fat collects in the neck", "The thyroid becomes infected", "Blood vessels burst"], 0, "Lymph nodes contain lymphocytes. When they detect invaders they multiply rapidly, enlarging the node."],
    [4, "How does fat from your food first enter the circulation?", ["Directly into the liver's blood supply", "Through the stomach wall", "Through the skin", "Through lacteals in the lymphatic system"], 3, "Fats are packed into chylomicrons that enter lacteals, travel up the thoracic duct and join the blood near the collarbone."],
    [6, "What did researchers discover in 2015?", ["The brain has no lymphatic vessels", "Lymphatic vessels in the membranes surrounding the brain", "A second thoracic duct", "That lymph nodes produce blood"], 1, "Two teams independently found lymphatic vessels in the meninges, overturning the long-held belief that the brain had none."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("vestigial-body-parts", "body", "intermediate", "Evolution's leftovers: goosebumps, tailbones and the appendix", "Your body carries traces of ancestors who had fur, tails and much bigger jaws. Some leftovers have found new jobs; others are just along for the ride.",
  [
    ["What vestigial really means", "A vestigial structure is reduced or has lost its original function, but it is not necessarily useless.",
      "In The Descent of Man, published in 1871, Charles Darwin pointed to what he called rudimentary organs as evidence that humans share ancestry with other animals. These were body parts that seemed shrunken or purposeless in us but were fully functional in other species. The modern term is vestigial. It is often misunderstood as meaning completely useless, but biologists use it more carefully: a vestigial structure has lost its original function, or much of it, though it may have gained a new one. In the late 1800s some anatomists drew up long lists of supposedly useless organs. Some items on those lists, such as the thymus, later turned out to be essential."],
    ["Goosebumps", "Goosebumps are a reflex that fluffs up fur, which is useful for furry mammals and nearly pointless for us.",
      "When you are cold or deeply moved by music, tiny muscles at the base of your hair follicles contract, pulling the hairs upright and raising little bumps on your skin. Each of these arrector pili muscles is attached to a single hair. In heavily furred mammals the same reflex fluffs up the coat, trapping a thicker layer of insulating air. It also makes an animal look bigger, which is why a frightened or angry cat puffs up. Humans have lost most of our body fur, so the reflex now achieves very little warmth or intimidation. Researchers have found these muscles may still play a supporting role in hair growth, but the visible goosebump is largely a leftover."],
    ["The tail you once had", "Every human embryo briefly has a tail, and the tailbone is what remains.",
      "At the base of your spine sits the coccyx, or tailbone, made from several small vertebrae fused together. It is what remains of the tail our primate ancestors carried, and apes such as chimpanzees and gorillas have one too. Human embryos make the connection vivid. Around the fourth to sixth week of development, an embryo has a distinct tail with more vertebrae than an adult eventually keeps. Over the following weeks the tail stops growing while the rest of the body catches up, and its extra cells are removed. Yet the coccyx is not useless. It anchors muscles, tendons and ligaments of the pelvic floor, and it bears some of your weight when you lean back to sit."],
    ["The appendix rethought", "Once dismissed as useless, the appendix appears to support the immune system and gut bacteria.",
      "Darwin suggested the appendix was a shrunken remnant of a larger organ that helped ancestral primates digest leaves. For more than a century it was treated as a useless troublemaker, famous mainly for appendicitis, which can be life-threatening if the organ bursts. The picture has changed. The appendix is rich in lymphoid tissue, involved in immune defence. Researchers have also proposed that it acts as a safe house for beneficial gut bacteria, helping repopulate the intestine after an illness such as severe diarrhoea. Comparative studies suggest an appendix has evolved independently many times in different mammals, which hints that it offers some advantage. Even so, people who have it removed live normal, healthy lives."],
    ["Too many teeth", "Wisdom teeth often do not fit because human jaws have become smaller over evolutionary time.",
      "Our ancestors had larger jaws and chewed tougher, rougher food that wore their teeth down. Over hundreds of thousands of years, human jaws and faces became smaller, a change many researchers link to cooking and softer diets. The teeth did not shrink as much, and third molars, known as wisdom teeth, often arrive to find there is no room. They can come in crooked or stay trapped in the jaw, which is called impaction, and cause pain or infection. Some researchers also think soft modern diets mean jaws grow a little less during childhood. A significant minority of people never develop one or more wisdom teeth at all, which may be evolution slowly tidying up."],
    ["Muscles you may not have", "Some people lack the palmaris longus muscle in their forearm and never notice any difference.",
      "Touch the tip of your thumb to the tip of your little finger and bend your wrist slightly forwards. Many people will see a thin cord rise in the middle of the inner wrist. That is the tendon of the palmaris longus, a muscle that seems to have been more useful to tree-climbing ancestors. Roughly one in seven people, a figure that varies between populations, lack it in one or both arms, with no noticeable loss of grip strength. Surgeons find it handy as a spare part, harvesting the tendon to repair others. Similarly, most people have small muscles around their ears that some animals use to swivel them towards sounds. A few humans can still wiggle their ears."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What do biologists mean by a vestigial structure?", ["One that is always completely useless", "One that has lost its original function, or much of it", "One that only appears in embryos", "One that grows larger with age"], 1, "Vestigial means a structure has lost all or much of its original function. It may still have, or have gained, other uses."],
    [1, "What do goosebumps do for furry mammals?", ["Fluff up the fur for insulation and to look bigger", "Help them sweat", "Make their skin waterproof", "Help them hear better"], 0, "The reflex raises fur, trapping insulating air and making the animal look larger. Humans lack the fur for it to do much."],
    [2, "What useful job does the coccyx still do?", ["It produces blood cells", "It controls balance", "It anchors muscles and ligaments of the pelvic floor", "It protects the kidneys"], 2, "The tailbone anchors pelvic floor muscles, tendons and ligaments and helps bear weight when sitting back."],
    [3, "What role have researchers proposed for the appendix?", ["Digesting cellulose in humans", "Storing fat", "Making digestive enzymes", "A safe house for beneficial gut bacteria"], 3, "The appendix contains immune tissue and may shelter helpful bacteria that can repopulate the gut after illness."],
    [5, "What happens to people who lack the palmaris longus muscle?", ["They lose much of their grip strength", "They cannot bend their wrist", "They notice no meaningful difference", "They cannot wiggle their ears"], 2, "Around one in seven people lack it in one or both arms, with no noticeable loss of grip, and surgeons use the tendon as a spare part."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("what-cancer-is", "body", "advanced", "What cancer really is", "Cancer is not one disease but hundreds, all sharing a single rebellion: cells that stop following the body's rules. Understanding that rebellion has transformed treatment.",
  [
    ["An ancient enemy", "Cancer has been described for thousands of years, and its name comes from the Greek word for crab.",
      "Cancer is not a modern disease. An ancient Egyptian medical text known as the Edwin Smith Papyrus, written around 1600 BCE and probably copied from older sources, describes bulging masses in the breast and states that there is no treatment. Tumours have been identified in ancient mummies and skeletons. The Greek physician Hippocrates used the word karkinos, meaning crab, perhaps because swollen veins around some tumours reminded observers of a crab's legs, and the Romans translated it into the Latin word cancer. Today we know it is not one disease but hundreds, varying by the tissue and the genes involved. What unites them is cells that divide without control and can invade other tissues."],
    ["Accelerators and brakes", "Cancer begins when mutations damage genes that drive cell growth or genes that restrain it.",
      "Healthy cells divide only when they receive the right signals and stop when they should. This is governed by genes. Some, called proto-oncogenes, act like accelerators, promoting growth. When mutated, they can become oncogenes, like an accelerator stuck to the floor. Others, called tumour suppressor genes, act like brakes. The most famous is TP53, often called the guardian of the genome, which can halt division or order a damaged cell to destroy itself. It is mutated in around half of all cancers. In 1971 the geneticist Alfred Knudson proposed, from studying a childhood eye cancer, that both copies of a tumour suppressor gene usually need to be knocked out, an idea known as the two-hit hypothesis."],
    ["The hallmarks", "Cancer cells share a set of acquired abilities that scientists call the hallmarks of cancer.",
      "In 2000 the researchers Douglas Hanahan and Robert Weinberg published an influential paper arguing that, despite their variety, cancers share a handful of acquired capabilities. They listed six: producing their own growth signals, ignoring signals to stop dividing, avoiding programmed cell death, dividing indefinitely, building new blood vessels to feed themselves, and invading nearby tissue and spreading. In 2011 they updated the list, adding reprogramming how cells use energy and evading destruction by the immune system. They also highlighted enabling traits such as genetic instability and inflammation, and further refinements followed in 2022. No single mutation provides all these abilities. A cell usually accumulates them gradually, over years or decades, as one error builds on another."],
    ["The immortal cells", "Cancer cells escape the normal limit on how many times a cell can divide.",
      "In the early 1960s the biologist Leonard Hayflick showed that normal human cells grown in the laboratory divide only a limited number of times, roughly 40 to 60, before stopping. Part of the reason is telomeres, protective caps on the ends of chromosomes that shorten with each division. Most cancer cells avoid this limit, often by switching on an enzyme called telomerase that rebuilds the caps. The most famous example is HeLa cells, taken in 1951 from a tumour of Henrietta Lacks, an African American woman treated in Baltimore. Her cells were taken without her knowledge or consent, a common practice at the time. They have divided in laboratories ever since and contributed to countless discoveries, including the polio vaccine."],
    ["Evolution inside the body", "A tumour is an evolving population of cells, which is why cancers can become resistant to treatment.",
      "A tumour is not a uniform mass of identical cells. As it grows, its cells keep picking up new mutations, so a single tumour can contain many genetically different groups. This makes cancer a case of evolution by natural selection happening inside one person. Any cell that happens to grow faster, survive with less oxygen or resist a drug will tend to leave more descendants. This explains one of the hardest problems in treatment. A therapy may shrink a tumour dramatically, only for a resistant population to survive and regrow. It is one reason doctors often combine treatments, attacking cancer cells in several ways at once. Most deaths from cancer are caused by tumours that have spread, or metastasised."],
    ["Age, luck and causes", "Cancer risk rises with age as mutations accumulate, and some causes are well established.",
      "Every time a cell divides, there is a small chance of copying errors, and our DNA is also damaged by outside factors. Over a lifetime, mutations accumulate, which is the main reason cancer becomes more common with age. Some causes are firmly established. Tobacco smoke is linked to many cancers, not only lung cancer. Ultraviolet radiation causes most skin cancers, a serious issue in sunny Australia. Alcohol raises the risk of several cancers, and some viruses, such as human papillomavirus, cause cancer. Curiously, large animals do not get more cancer than small ones, despite having more cells, a puzzle called Peto's paradox. Elephants, for example, have many extra copies of the TP53 gene."],
    ["Fighting back", "Treatments now range from surgery and radiation to drugs that target specific mutations and unleash the immune system.",
      "Surgery, radiation and chemotherapy remain central to cancer treatment, but newer approaches build directly on understanding how cancer works. Targeted therapies block the specific molecules a cancer depends on. A landmark example was imatinib, approved in 2001, which transformed the outlook for a blood cancer called chronic myeloid leukaemia. Immunotherapy releases the brakes that cancers use to hide from immune cells. James Allison and Tasuku Honjo shared the 2018 Nobel Prize for discoveries behind these checkpoint drugs. Prevention matters too. Australia's human papillomavirus vaccination program and cervical screening have put the country on track to eliminate cervical cancer as a public health problem. National screening programs also look for early breast and bowel cancers."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "What do tumour suppressor genes normally do?", ["Speed up cell division", "Repair broken bones", "Act as brakes on cell growth", "Produce growth signals"], 2, "Tumour suppressor genes such as TP53 restrain division or trigger the destruction of damaged cells. Losing them removes the brakes."],
    [2, "What were the hallmarks of cancer proposed by Hanahan and Weinberg?", ["A list of cancer symptoms", "Shared abilities that cancer cells acquire", "Stages of cancer treatment", "Types of cancer-causing viruses"], 1, "The hallmarks are capabilities cancer cells gain, such as sustaining growth signals, avoiding cell death and invading tissue."],
    [3, "How do many cancer cells escape the limit on cell divisions?", ["By activating telomerase to rebuild telomeres", "By shrinking their chromosomes", "By dividing more slowly", "By absorbing other cells' DNA"], 0, "Normal cells stop after roughly 40 to 60 divisions as telomeres shorten. Many cancer cells switch on telomerase to rebuild them."],
    [4, "Why can a cancer become resistant to a drug that first shrank it?", ["The drug loses strength in the body", "The patient's immune system blocks the drug", "Cancer cells learn from the drug", "Resistant cells survive and multiply, as in natural selection"], 3, "Tumours contain genetically varied cells. Treatment kills the vulnerable ones, leaving any resistant cells to regrow."],
    [5, "What is Peto's paradox?", ["Smokers who never get cancer", "Large animals do not get more cancer despite having more cells", "Cancer is more common in young people", "Tumours shrink without treatment"], 1, "More cells should mean more chances for cancer, yet large animals like elephants do not have higher rates, partly thanks to extra TP53 copies."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });
